# Command zed

## What does this command do:

Zed's CLI to open files and projects from the terminal.

## Installed from

The `zed` binary is the CLI — there is no separate CLI-only package. It is the
same executable that also launches the GUI editor (same pattern as VS Code's
`code` command). Running `zed` with no arguments or with a path opens a GUI
window by design; flags like `--version`/`--help` print straight to the
terminal with no window.

For Fedora install using: curl -f https://zed.dev/install.sh | sh
For Mac install using: brew install --cask zed
For Mac container (Fedora Linux, ARM/aarch64) install using: curl -f https://zed.dev/install.sh | sh

> Note: the Mac container runs Fedora Linux on ARM (aarch64), not x86_64. The
> install script auto-detects the architecture and fetches the aarch64 build;
> no separate arch-specific step is needed here.

**Raspberry Pi: intentionally not installed/documented.** Zed requires a
Vulkan-compatible GPU, and its docs list generic 64-bit ARM support but do not
mention Raspberry Pi. The RPi5's VideoCore VII GPU has known incomplete/unstable
Vulkan support, so a Zed install there is unlikely to actually run even though
the aarch64 binary would technically install. Decision made 2026-09-15; revisit
if RPi5 Vulkan support matures.

## Command path

For Fedora run: ~/.local/bin/zed
For Mac run: /opt/homebrew/bin/zed
For Mac container run: ~/.local/bin/zed

## Command documentation

Docs can be found at https://zed.dev/docs/reference/cli

## Command options

zed [OPTIONS] [PATHS_WITH_POSITION]...

  [PATHS_WITH_POSITION]...   Paths to open (space-separated). Use
                             path:line:column to open at a given line/column.
  -w, --wait                 Wait for all given paths to be opened/closed
                              before exiting (useful as $EDITOR)
  -a, --add                  Add files to the currently open workspace
  -n, --new                  Create a new workspace
  -e, --existing             Open in existing Zed window
      --user-data-dir DIR    Custom directory for user data (db, extensions, logs)
  -v, --version               Print Zed's version and the app path
      --foreground            Run in foreground (shows all logs, for debugging)
      --zed ZED                Custom path to Zed.app or the zed binary
      --dev-container          Open the project in a dev container
      --help                   Show the full help and exit

  # Common usage
  zed                       # open Zed (GUI window)
  zed .                     # open the current directory as a project
  zed file.txt              # open a specific file
  zed file.txt:42:10        # open at line 42, column 10
  zed -n path/to/project    # force a new window
  zed -w file.txt           # wait until closed (e.g. as $EDITOR)
  zed -v                    # print version, no GUI
  zed --help                # print usage, no GUI
